WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.460
QUESTION: A lot of Ursula Le Guin's world-building
is pretty political like The Dispossessed...

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.760
How did that influence you?

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.740
And was that something you ever discussed?

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.920
And do you think it explicitly colored
your approach to the Mars Trilogy?

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.100
Kim Stanley Robinson: Well, thank you for that.
Yes, yes, and yes.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.700
I'm glad you brought it up. Because if I'd had more time I'd have talked about it. And I will now.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.920
Ursula's politics were American leftist
of a different variety than mine.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.400
But we were both the kind of American leftist
that said, look, the front is broad

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.060
and we just take our different points of view and work together rather than the narcissism of small differences

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.120
where the leftists fight leftists because they're the only people that understand the nature of the argument.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.140
The Dispossessed is an anarchist text.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.880
And it is an interesting, compelling thought experiment:

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.260
What would it be like to live in an anarchist society?

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.280
Because really anarchism is almost politically autistic

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.220
where everybody's free and in everybody free contract between everybody and there are no rules...

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:17.540
She actually wrote an email to thank me
for my introduction to Magpie Killjoy's

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.160
anarchist (book "Mythmakers &amp; Lawbreakers").
I wrote the introduction.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.740
I said, anarchy can mean
"no rules" or "no ruler" in the Greek.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.540
I say it means no ruler because you have to have rules.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.440
And even on Anarres,
there's rules in the anarchistic society.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.180
It's like management of the Commons.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.600
The Commons was not a space where
you could do anything.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.100
It was managed and regulated.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:45.400
And anarchism as I understand it will be a complete horizontalization of power and wealth.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.520
That's the anarchist goal. A distant horizon
that everybody should be shooting for.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.300
So, she and I came at it from our different angles.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.570
I'm much more of a Marxist-Leninist, and a statist.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.619
Her anarchism, as she told me,
came out of the Tao Te Ching.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:05.440
Her translation of the Tao Te Ching is fabulously good.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.420
You need to cross-reference it with three or four others to realize how amorphous and murky that document is.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.960
But she comes to anarchism from the Tao.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.220
And from that strain of Chinese literature that I think comes also from her father

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.920
and the anthropological strain.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.960
I stayed up all night to read The Dispossessed.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.100
I had a little science fiction library club hardback. It was short and light. I got to what I call the Dickinson point.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.820
Which is the point where you know you have
to finish this book in that sitting.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.180
It comes from Peter Dickinson, who does it all the time.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.900
But I got to that point with her and it was 1AM.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.600
So I stayed up to like 4AM to read The Dispossessed.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:50.320
And when I was done, I was thinking: you can write a utopian novel and make it work as a novel.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.290
And that blew my mind.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:57.140
That was 1975.
And it was a huge impact on the Mars Trilogy.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.720
And everything that I've done since.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.780
Probably in terms of the content--
rather than the form--the content.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.340
Her influence comes to me mostly from Left Hand of Darkness and from The Dispossessed.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.520
As gender + politics...

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:20.860
She was really important and I know still is to anarchists and American leftists who are thinking about it.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.080
So thank you.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:37.000
McElligott: When you look at Left Hand of Darkness.
It's a novel about refugees and culture clash.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.160
It's a novel about gender and levels of trust.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:56.060
It's a novel where a society sees a potential to progress that's offered and isn't sure if it's going to take the offer.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:06.500
I mean two societies on that planet. So it's an interesting foil for the moment we're in now.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.640
Robinson: Left Hand of Darkness is a great novel.
I would say it's her masterpiece.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.800
And one of the great novels of the 20th century.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.280
And it has to do with a couple of things.
Karhide is feudal.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.660
The other society, on the other side of the world,
is socialist.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.960
In a bad Soviet totalitarian way.
There isn't a capitalist society.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.520
There's only these two struggling.
And both of them mired in their problems.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.260
So it has a lot of interesting political thoughts.
I think she was thinking...

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:40.020
that maybe the two of them together was a portrait of the United States in the 1960s.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:47.000
Partly feudal and partly bad socialism.
But no function. No real culture.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:56.060
And then the gender thing for me was that
most of the time gender isn't important for social life.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.900
Sometimes it is. Pay attention to that.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.340
And when my wife and I had our first kid...

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:10.480
she's a scientist working 12 hours a day, six days a week. And when my boy was 10-weeks old

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.500
I took over. And was a caretaker of this child.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.320
Mr. Mom as they put it
in the great movie by Michael Keaton.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.520
I was Mr. Mom for years.
In The Left Hand of Darkness this idea

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.960
the king was pregnant, which obviously I wasn't.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.880
But the King did a lot of bottle feedings.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.500
They were very maternal in their affect.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.680
And I thought, yeah man.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.500
Left Hand helped me to conceptualize
the feelings that I had.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.380
The structure of feeling had changed

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.640
because of that book.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:50.720
So I was just in... kemmer had come along
and I was in my female existence.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.740
And I was really happy. I thought I was pretty good at it.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.900
This is profound, what literature can do for you.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.660
And she knew that.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.300
I think she knew that she had hit it
out of the park with that book.

